Device mutation site permission caps
====================================

Legacy device deletion, reboot, locate, LED, adoption, forgetting and firmware
upgrade now enforce their existing route permission at the resolved device site
before any adapter is constructed or state changes. Existing organization and
site visibility checks remain first, and role/API-key permission ceilings and
destructive-action confirmation remain unchanged. Insufficient site level gives
403. Read-only and invalid site levels cannot mutate devices; a write-level
site cannot delete, adopt, forget or batch-reboot under the existing admin/delete
permission classification. Firmware still requires firmware:upgrade at the route;
this change does not grant that permission to any role.

Batch routes pass their exact route permission to the shared helper. Each device
is checked before it enters a controller group. Mixed batches retain per-device
results, operate only on eligible devices, and do not construct an adapter when
none qualify. Individual and batch actions retain their existing result shapes.

Administrator, grant-less-user and null-level inheritance rules stay consistent
with CurrentUser.has_site_permission. Reads using the device loader remain
unchanged; the additional permission argument is explicit on mutation callers.
Other device modules, controller/credential resolution, lifecycle consistency,
concurrent principal revocation and sensitive adapter diagnostics are separate
work. Existing reference links are not rewritten.

Qualification uses actual PostgreSQL rows and HTTP-ASGI plus independent TCP
loopback HTTP. The authenticated principal and controller adapters are synthetic;
the actual permission dependency, endpoint, batch grouping and database commits
execute. Checks cover read/write/admin/inherited/invalid caps, scoped read keys,
organization admins, grant-less users, inaccessible devices, confirmations and
mixed batches. Denials perform no adapter construction, no adapter call and no
database mutation. Accepted fake-adapter calls and closure counts are checked.
This does not prove hardware command execution, real login, concurrent authority
revocation or enterprise operational acceptance. Shipping-image checks are
separately gated and recorded.
